    "https://employment.niu.edu/postings/97686": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Criminology (6749)",
      "description": "Northern Illinois University | Assistant Professor of Criminology (6749) Skip to Main Content Find your Career at Northern Illinois University! Toggle navigation Home Search All Jobs Log In /Create Account Help NIU Human Resources Assistant Professor of Criminology (6749) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Working Position Title Assistant Professor of Criminology (6749) Classification Title Assistant Professor Position Number 00006749 Posting Date 10/08/2026 Closing Date Priority Review Date 01/10/2027 Business Unit Description Faculty Full-Time/Part Time Full-Time FTE 1.00 Position Status Regular FLSA Exempt Job Code 2000 Union Representation Union Union Code UFA - United Faculty Alliance Unit/Department Sociology and Criminology College/Unit College of LA&S Dean Division Academic Division Location ZH 815 Link to Department Website or Custom Search Page Department of Sociology and Criminology Position Summary, Requirements, and Qualifications Position Summary, Requirements, and Qualifications Overview The Department of Sociology and Criminology at Northern Illinois University ( NIU ) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Criminology to start August 2027. The successful candidate will contribute to the department’s undergraduate criminology curriculum by teaching criminological theory, quantitative and/or qualitative research methods, and other courses that support the growing criminology major and minor. The department also offers a terminal Master of Arts degree in Sociology, including an emphasis in criminology.Preferred areas of teaching interest include the criminal justice system, violence, and policing. Areas of research specialization are open. Northern Illinois University is a comprehensive research university offering programs from the bachelor’s through doctoral levels across seven academic colleges. Located in DeKalb, Illinois, approximately 65 miles west of Chicago, NIU serves approximately 16,000 students and combines the advantages of a vibrant university community with access to the cultural, professional, and recreational opportunities of the greater Chicago region. Position Summary The Assistant Professor of Criminology is a tenure-track faculty position responsible for teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, maintaining an active research agenda, mentoring students, supervising student research, and contributing service to the department, university, and profession. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Teaching undergraduate and graduate courses Building and maintaining a research agenda in areas of specialization Supervising student projects, independent studies, and theses Mentoring students Engaging in service to the department, college, university, and profession Minimum Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Sociology, Criminology, or related field by the start date of appointment Additional Requirements N/A Preferred Qualifications Evidence of scholarly productivity commensurate with experience, including research focused on crime, the criminal justice system, violence, or policing. Ability to teach undergraduate-level punishment and corrections, criminological theory, and quantitative or qualitative research methods courses. Demonstrable commitment to teaching and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students from diverse backgrounds. Physical Demands Summary For physical demands and requirements please see human resources policies and protocols. Appointment and Compensation Details Appointment and Compensation Details Anticipated Appointment Start Date 08/16/2027 Anticipated Appointment End Date Percent of Staff Year 0.75 Standard Hours Per Week 37.50 Salary Starting at $77,000. Faculty compensation is governed by the UFA CBA . NIU offers a robust benefits package. Hourly or Semi-monthly Pay Rate/Range Starting at $4,277.78 per pay period. Faculty compensation is governed by the UFA CBA. NIU offers a robust benefits package . Earn Type Salary Application Procedure Application Procedure Application Procedure For full consideration, prospective applicants should visit Assistant Professor of Criminology to create an application profile and submit the following materials by January 10, 2027: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research agenda (1-2 pages) Teaching statement (1-2 pages) Names and contact information for three professional references (letters of recommendation may be requested later in the search process) Unofficial graduate transcripts For questions, contact the search committee chair Jeff Kidder at jkidder@niu.edu . Official graduate transcripts and the successful completion of a background check will be required upon hire. Special Instructions Quick Link to Applicant https://employment.niu.edu/postings/97684 Is Background Check Required Yes EEO Statement Summary In accordance with applicable statutes and regulations, NIU is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, religion, age, disability (physical and mental), marital status, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender (identity and expression), political affiliation, or any other factor unrelated to professional qualifications, and will comply with all applicable federal and state statutes, regulations and orders pertaining to nondiscrimination, equal opportunity and affirmative action. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the non-discrimination policies: Director of Civil Rights Office, Title IX Coordinator Health Services 2nd floor CivilRights@niu.edu 815-753-0455 Visa Policy In compliance with federal law, all persons hired will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification document form upon hire. ADA Accommodation NIU remains committed to ensuring that its recruitment and application procedures include full opportunities for applicants with disabilities. Employment opportunities will not be denied to anyone because of the need to make accommodations for a person’s disability during either the application or interview process. An applicant who believes they require an accommodation to participate in the employment process due to a disability may request that accommodation through the Accommodation Request Form . For further assistance, please contact the office of Affirmative Action and Equal Opportunity ( AAEO ) at ada@niu.edu . Safety Information NIU provides annual reports on campus security and fire safety. Read the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Contact the Ethics and Compliance Office at 815-753-9364 for a hard copy. Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume/Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement List of at least 3 Professional References Transcripts (unofficial with official required at hire) Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). © 2019 Board of Trustees of Northern Illinois University All rights reserved. Back to Top Northern Illinois University 1425 W. Lincoln Hwy., DeKalb, IL 60115 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.niu.edu/postings/97676": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Public Administration - Local Government Management (29437)",
      "description": "Northern Illinois University | Assistant Professor of Public Administration - Local Government Management (29437) Skip to Main Content Find your Career at Northern Illinois University! Toggle navigation Home Search All Jobs Log In /Create Account Help NIU Human Resources Assistant Professor of Public Administration - Local Government Management (29437) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Details Position Information Working Position Title Assistant Professor of Public Administration - Local Government Management (29437) Classification Title Assistant Professor Position Number 00029437 Posting Date 10/08/2026 Closing Date Priority Review Date 11/16/2026 Business Unit Description Faculty Full-Time/Part Time Full-Time FTE 1.00 Position Status Regular FLSA Exempt Job Code 2000 Union Representation Union Union Code UFA - United Faculty Alliance Unit/Department Public Administration College/Unit College of LA&S Dean Division Academic Division Location IA 215 Link to Department Website or Custom Search Page Department of Public Administration Position Summary, Requirements, and Qualifications Position Summary, Requirements, and Qualifications Overview The Department of Public Administration in Northern Illinois University’s School of Public and Global Affairs invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning August 16, 2027. The department prepares students for public service through its nationally recognized, NASPAA -accredited Master of Public Administration program, the online Bachelor of Science in Public Service Leadership program, and graduate certificates, including the Graduate Certificate in Public Budgeting and Financial Management. The department has longstanding relationships with local governments and public-service organizations in the greater Chicago metropolitan area, Illinois, the Midwest, and nationally. We seek a colleague whose teaching and scholarship will strengthen the department’s established areas of distinction in local government management and public budgeting and financial management. The successful candidate will contribute to graduate and undergraduate instruction, maintain an active program of scholarly research, advise and mentor students, and participate meaningfully in service and professionally engaged activities consistent with the department’s public-service mission. Northern Illinois University is a comprehensive research university serving approximately 16,000 students and offering programs from the baccalaureate through doctoral levels. The university’s main campus is located in DeKalb, Illinois, approximately 65 miles west of Chicago, with additional education centers in Naperville and Rockford. The region offers a blend of cultural, recreational, and professional opportunities with convenient access to the greater Chicago metropolitan area. Position Summary The successful candidate will develop and sustain a program of high-quality scholarly research in local government management, local governance, public budgeting and financial management, or closely related areas. The candidate will teach graduate and undergraduate courses in the department’s programs, with particular responsibility for courses in local government management and the ability to contribute to public budgeting or public financial management. Teaching assignments may include in-person, hybrid, and online courses. The successful candidate will also advise and mentor students; contribute to departmental, school, college, university, and professional service; support program assessment and accreditation activities; and develop productive relationships with public-service organizations and professional associations. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Develop and maintain an active scholarly research agenda leading to peer-reviewed publications and other appropriate forms of scholarly dissemination in local government management, public budgeting, public finance, or a related area, broadly conceived. Pursue external funding, contracts, collaborative research, or other forms of professional engagement as appropriate to the candidate’s research agenda and consistent with departmental standards. Teach graduate and undergraduate courses in public administration, public management, and public service. Contribute to instruction in local government management, public budgeting, public finance, and related areas. Deliver effective instruction in the formats and at the locations needed by the department, which may include in-person, hybrid, online asynchronous, and online synchronous instruction. Advise and mentor graduate and undergraduate students, including students preparing for careers in public and nonprofit service. Build and sustain relationships with local governments, professional associations, other public-service organizations, and alumni. Participate in department, school, college, university, and professional service. Contribute to curriculum development, student learning assessment, and activities supporting NASPAA accreditation and continuous program improvement. Minimum Required Qualifications An earned doctorate in Public Administration, Public Policy, Public Affairs, Planning, Political Science, or a closely related field by the date of appointment. A substantive research specialization in local government management, public budgeting, public finance, or a related area, broadly conceived. Evidence of, or strong potential for, a sustained program of peer-reviewed scholarly research. Evidence of, or strong potential for, an ability to teach at a university level in public administration. Evidence of, or strong potential to develop, an ability to contribute to both graduate and undergraduate education. Additional Requirements Ability to teach effectively in-person and online courses as assigned. Effective written and oral communication skills. Ability to work constructively and collegially with students, faculty, staff, alumni, and public-service partners. Ability to advise and mentor students from a range of educational, professional, and personal backgrounds. Commitment to professional ethics, academic integrity, NIU’s public-service mission, and the Department’s mission. Preferred Qualifications A consistent record of scholarly research, conference presentations, and publications addressing local government management, public budgeting, public finance, or a related area, broadly conceived. Experience teaching courses in local government management, public budgeting, public finance, or related areas, in a supportive and/or primary instructional capacity. Experience teaching working professionals and adult learners. Experience developing and/or delivering high-quality online instruction. Professional experience in, or a record of collaboration with, local governments, local government associations, or other public-service organizations. Evidence of the ability to develop applied research supported by grants and contracts. Ability to contribute to complementary departmental strengths in sustainability policy and management, nonprofit management, strategic public management and leadership, and/or emergency management and public safety. Engagement with relevant professional or scholarly associations. Physical Demands Summary For physical demands and requirements please see human resources policies and protocols. Appointment and Compensation Details Appointment and Compensation Details Anticipated Appointment Start Date 08/16/2027 Anticipated Appointment End Date Percent of Staff Year 0.75 Standard Hours Per Week 37.50 Salary Starting at $75,000. Faculty compensation is governed by the UFA CBA . NIU offers a robust benefits package. Hourly or Semi-monthly Pay Rate/Range Starting at $4,166.67 per pay period. Faculty compensation is governed by the UFA CBA. NIU offers a robust benefits package . Earn Type Salary Application Procedure Application Procedure Application Procedure Interested candidates should visit Assistant Professor of Public Administration – Local Government Management to create an application profile and submit the following materials by November 16, 2026 : Cover letter describing the applicant’s qualifications for the position and interest in NIU’s Department of Public Administration Curriculum vitae Statement of research agenda and plans for future scholarship Statement of teaching philosophy and experience, including experience or interest in graduate, undergraduate, online, hybrid, and professionally oriented instruction One representative scholarly writing sample Unofficial graduate transcripts. Official transcripts will be required upon appointment Names and contact information for three professional references Letters of recommendation may be requested later in the search process. For full consideration, completed applications must be received by November 16, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Special Instructions Quick Link to Applicant https://employment.niu.edu/postings/97675 Is Background Check Required Yes EEO Statement Summary In accordance with applicable statutes and regulations, NIU is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, religion, age, disability (physical and mental), marital status, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender (identity and expression), political affiliation, or any other factor unrelated to professional qualifications, and will comply with all applicable federal and state statutes, regulations and orders pertaining to nondiscrimination, equal opportunity and affirmative action. The following person has been designated to handle inquiries regarding the non-discrimination policies: Director of Civil Rights Office, Title IX Coordinator Health Services 2nd floor CivilRights@niu.edu 815-753-0455 Visa Policy In compliance with federal law, all persons hired will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification document form upon hire. ADA Accommodation NIU remains committed to ensuring that its recruitment and application procedures include full opportunities for applicants with disabilities. Employment opportunities will not be denied to anyone because of the need to make accommodations for a person’s disability during either the application or interview process. An applicant who believes they require an accommodation to participate in the employment process due to a disability may request that accommodation through the Accommodation Request Form . For further assistance, please contact the office of Affirmative Action and Equal Opportunity ( AAEO ) at ada@niu.edu . Safety Information NIU provides annual reports on campus security and fire safety. Read the Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . Contact the Ethics and Compliance Office at 815-753-9364 for a hard copy. Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Resume/Curriculum Vitae Statement of Research Vision Statement of Teaching Philosophy Writing Sample Transcripts (unofficial with official required at hire) List of at least 3 Professional References Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). © 2019 Board of Trustees of Northern Illinois University All rights reserved. Back to Top Northern Illinois University 1425 W. Lincoln Hwy., DeKalb, IL 60115 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
